# NEAR AI Cloud: the upsides and downsides of using it as your inference provider

*Notes after wiring it in as a provider across three coding agents.*

NEAR AI Cloud is an OpenAI-compatible inference gateway that puts frontier
models (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google) and open ones (Qwen, GLM, DeepSeek, Kimi)
behind a single key and a single URL. The catalog isn't the differentiator —
OpenRouter already does that. What is: inference runs inside TEEs (Intel TDX
plus NVIDIA's GPU-level TEE), and you can ask for a cryptographic attestation
that it actually did.

I'm writing this after pointing three different agents at it — **OpenClaw**,
**Hermes**, and **Pi** — not after reading the landing page. Both sides below
are real.

The short version: **it just works.** All three agents ran against it with a
base URL and a key, no patches, no shims. What's missing isn't reliability —
it's surface area: no TTS, no realtime voice, no video. More on that below.

---

## What's good

### 1. It's a literal OpenAI drop-in

`base_url = "https://cloud-api.near.ai/v1"` plus your key. The OpenAI SDK,
LangChain, LiteLLM — anything that speaks Chat Completions works without
touching anything else.

Concretely, across the three agents:

- **OpenClaw** — `openclaw onboard --classic`, pick a custom provider, paste
  the base URL and the key. Because the model IDs are vendor-prefixed
  (`anthropic/claude-sonnet-5`, not `claude-sonnet-5`), you type the full
  prefixed ID when it asks for the model. That's the one gotcha.
- **Hermes** — same story: OpenAI-compatible endpoint, `NEARAI_API_KEY`, done.
- **Pi** — same again.

Three agents, three configs, zero code changes. That's the actual headline.

### 2. Verifiable privacy, not promised privacy

This is the part traditional providers have no equivalent for. Everyone says
"we don't train on your data"; here there's an endpoint —
`GET /v1/attestation/report` — that returns the enclave's attestation. The gap
between a privacy policy and a signed hardware quote is the gap between
trusting and verifying.

Who actually needs it: health, legal, fintech — anyone who today *can't* send
the data to an LLM for compliance reasons. For a side project it's irrelevant,
and you shouldn't pay complexity for it.

### 3. A mixed catalog with list prices

48 entries today, ~42 of them conversational, and the public catalog
(`/v1/models`, no key needed) ships per-model pricing and metadata. A sample:

| Model | Context | $/M in | $/M out |
|---|---|---|---|
| `anthropic/claude-sonnet-5` | 1M | 3.00 | 15.00 |
| `openai/gpt-5.4` | 1.05M | 2.50 | 15.00 |
| `google/gemini-2.5-flash` | 1M | 0.30 | 2.50 |
| `z-ai/glm-5.2` | 1.05M | 1.40 | 4.40 |
| `moonshotai/kimi-k2.6` | 262k | 0.81 | 3.85 |
| `deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash` | 1.05M | 0.17 | 0.35 |
| `openai/gpt-5-nano` | 400k | 0.05 | 0.40 |

Frontier prices sit level with the original provider's list — you're not paying
a visible toll for the TEE. And the range is wide enough to matter: Sonnet in
the main loop, DeepSeek-V4-Flash (0.17/0.35) on the mechanical work, one key,
one SDK.

### 4. Out of the walled garden without changing code

One client to try Qwen, GLM and Kimi against GPT and Claude, same request
shape. Picking a model per task — which is where every serious agent ends up —
is the concrete operational win, TEE aside.

---

## What's missing

### 1. Text and images only: no TTS, no realtime voice, no video

This is the biggest gap, and it's a gap in coverage rather than in quality.
What's there works; what isn't there, isn't there yet:

- **No TTS.** There's ASR (`whisper-large-v3`) for speech in, but nothing for
  speech out. Any voice UX still needs a second provider.
- **No realtime / streaming voice session API.** No equivalent of OpenAI's
  Realtime or Anthropic's bidirectional streaming, so no low-latency
  conversational voice.
- **No video.** Not in, not out.

Images are the exception and they're fully covered: vision input works on the
multimodal chat models, and generation is there too
(`black-forest-labs/FLUX.2-klein-4B`). So text and images are done — voice and
video are the open items.

If you're building a text or image agent, none of this touches you. If you want
voice or video, NEAR AI Cloud covers the reasoning and you bring the rest.

### 2. Latency overhead, and one more hop

The docs themselves quote ~5–10% extra latency. Acceptable, but real — and it
comes on top of there now being one more intermediary between your app and the
model. When something breaks you have a third place for the problem to live and
a third status page to check.

### 3. It's a catalog of *products*, not chat models

This was the concrete bug that cost me time. `/v1/models` is not a list of
conversational models: it mixes an image generator, an ASR model
(`whisper-large-v3`, context 448), an embedder, a reranker and a 512-token
privacy filter in with the 42 chat models. Any integration doing
`[m["id"] for m in data]` — which is most of them — will happily offer a
diffusion model in the chat picker.

Reading `input_modalities` / `output_modalities` / `context_length` fixes it,
but you have to know to do it. And rerankers aren't distinguishable from
metadata at all: they advertise as text→text with a normal context, so there
you're stuck filtering by ID.

### 4. Per-model metadata doesn't always match the model

`anthropic/claude-opus-4-6` is listed as `input_modalities: ["text"]`, no
image, when the upstream model does see images. Output ceilings aren't
normalized across families either. If your code derives capabilities from the
catalog, you'll want a per-model override hook — assuming the catalog is the
truth will bite you on the vision path.

### 5. Smaller API surface than going native

It's Chat Completions, and that's it. What you give up versus hitting the
provider directly: Anthropic's Messages API with its fine-grained cache
control, OpenAI's Responses API, batch, fine-tuning, and generally any new
feature arrives — if it arrives — late. The catalog does expose
`input_cache_read` on most models, so prompt caching exists, but the gateway
manages it, not you.

### 6. Maturity and concentration risk

It's a young provider. The API code is open source but under PolyForm Strict
1.0.0 — readable, not commercially usable, not forkable in production, so "it's
open source" is not an emergency exit. The public rate limits I found are on
the order of 100 req/s per tenant, plenty for everything short of real scale.
And it's one more dependency that could run out of funding: if your product
lives on this, have the fallback to the native provider *written*, not
imagined.

Small but annoying: `docs.near.ai` returns 403 to automated fetchers, so
neither your agent nor your scripts can read the documentation. In 2026 that
stings more than it sounds.

### 7. The TEE covers inference, not your architecture

Easy to misread. The attestation proves the computation ran in an enclave; it
says nothing about your logs, your conversation database, your telemetry, or
what your own code does with the prompt before sending it. If the rest of the
stack leaks, the TEE was decorative.

---

## When to use it, when not to

**Use it if** you have a real privacy requirement that blocks you today
(health, legal, fintech), or if you want one client to route between frontier
and open models and 5–10% latency doesn't move your needle.

**Don't** if you need Anthropic's or OpenAI's native features (Messages API,
manual caching, batch, fine-tuning), if you're fighting for milliseconds, if
you need voice or video in the same provider, or if "privacy" in your case
means marketing rather than compliance — there you're paying an intermediary
for nothing.

**The middle ground, which is where I landed:** it's worth having configured as
an alternate provider. Integration is cheap, there's one key, and the day the
compliance requirement shows up it's already running. Three agents in and the
text path has nothing I'd call a blocker — the roadmap items are TTS, realtime
voice and video, and they're additions, not fixes.

